Review this JavaScript snippet. Stated intent: sort contest scores numerically, lowest to highest.

```javascript
const scores = [10, 9, 80, 100];
scores.sort();
console.log(scores);
```

**Bug: the default `sort` is lexicographic, not numeric.** With no comparator, elements are converted to strings and ordered by UTF-16 code units, so "10" and "100" sort before "80", which sorts before "9". The array *looks* sorted at a glance — it starts with 10 — which is how this survives casual inspection.

Failing case: the snippet prints `[ 10, 100, 80, 9 ]`; numeric order should be `[ 9, 10, 80, 100 ]`.

Fix: supply a numeric comparator:

```javascript
const scores = [10, 9, 80, 100];
scores.sort((a, b) => a - b);
console.log(scores);
```

This prints `[ 9, 10, 80, 100 ]`. (Note `sort` also mutates in place; if the original order matters elsewhere, copy first with `[...scores]`.)

Code review, please — this JavaScript code is meant to: treat a form value as missing only when the field was left blank.

```javascript
function isMissing(v) {
  return v == "";
}
console.log(isMissing(0));
console.log(isMissing("0"));
```

**Bug: loose equality coerces the number 0 to the empty string.** With `==`, operands of different types are converted before comparison, and `0 == ""` is `true` (both become 0). A quantity field legitimately set to 0 is therefore reported as missing, while the string `"0"` is not — the check is wrong in both directions at once.

Failing case: the snippet prints `true` then `false`; the intent expects `false` for 0 (a real value) and `false` for "0", with `true` only for an actual blank.

Fix: compare strictly, and name what "blank" means:

```javascript
function isMissing(v) {
  return v === "" || v === null || v === undefined;
}
console.log(isMissing(0));
console.log(isMissing(""));
```

This prints `false` then `true`. Default to `===` everywhere; treat `==` as a deliberate, commented exception.

Review the following JavaScript code. It is supposed to: schedule three timers that each report their own index.

```javascript
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
```

**Bug: `var` gives the loop one shared binding, and the callbacks run after the loop ends.** `var i` is function-scoped, so all three arrow functions capture the *same* variable. `setTimeout` callbacks cannot fire until the synchronous loop finishes, by which time `i` is 3 — every timer reports the final value.

Failing case: the snippet prints `3`, `3`, `3`; the intent expects `0`, `1`, `2`.

Fix: use `let`, which creates a fresh binding per iteration:

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}
```

This prints `0`, `1`, `2`. (Wrapping the body in an IIFE that takes `i` as a parameter is the pre-`let` workaround and still appears in older codebases.)

Review this JavaScript snippet. Stated intent: fetch each id's doubled value from an async source and return all results once every fetch has finished.

```javascript
const fetchDoubled = (id) =>
  new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(id * 2), 1));

async function loadAll(ids) {
  const out = [];
  ids.forEach(async (id) => {
    out.push(await fetchDoubled(id));
  });
  return out;
}
loadAll([1, 2, 3]).then((r) => console.log(r));
```

**Bug: `forEach` ignores the promises its callback returns.** Each async callback starts, suspends at its `await`, and `forEach` moves on — nothing collects or waits for the callbacks. `loadAll` returns while `out` is still empty, and because an async function's promise settles as soon as it returns, the caller's `.then` runs with that empty array. The pushes do land a millisecond later, into an array nobody is looking at anymore.

Failing case: the snippet prints `[]`; the intent expects `[ 2, 4, 6 ]`.

Fix: collect the promises and await them together:

```javascript
const fetchDoubled = (id) =>
  new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(id * 2), 1));

async function loadAll(ids) {
  return Promise.all(ids.map((id) => fetchDoubled(id)));
}
loadAll([1, 2, 3]).then((r) => console.log(r));
```

This prints `[ 2, 4, 6 ]`, in input order. A `for...of` loop with `await` inside also works when the work must be sequential rather than concurrent.

