Review the following JavaScript code. It is supposed to: grant access only when the stored role is exactly admin.

```javascript
let role = "guest";
if ((role = "admin")) {
  console.log("granted:", role);
}
```

**Bug: assignment where a comparison was meant — `role = "admin"` instead of `role === "admin"`.** The condition *sets* role to "admin" and then tests the assigned value, which is a non-empty string and therefore truthy. The branch always executes, and as a side effect the variable has been overwritten, destroying the evidence of what the stored role actually was. Every user is granted, and the audit trail now says they were admin all along.

Failing case: the snippet prints `granted: admin` for a guest.

Fix: compare, and keep the comparison strict:

```javascript
let role = "guest";
if (role === "admin") {
  console.log("granted:", role);
}
```

This prints nothing. (Writing the literal first — `"admin" === role`, the old "Yoda condition" habit — makes the assignment form a syntax error, which is why some style guides still insist on it.)

Review this JavaScript snippet. Stated intent: return a user object built from the supplied name.

```javascript
function makeUser(name) {
  return
  { name: name };
}
console.log(makeUser("ana"));
```

**Bug: automatic semicolon insertion ends the `return` at the line break.** JavaScript inserts a semicolon after `return` when a newline follows, so the function returns `undefined` and the object literal on the next line is an unreachable expression statement. No error is raised — the code is syntactically valid and quietly wrong, which makes this one of the nastiest formatting-sensitive bugs in the language.

Failing case: the snippet prints `undefined`; the intent expects `{ name: 'ana' }`.

Fix: keep the opening brace on the `return` line:

```javascript
function makeUser(name) {
  return { name: name };
}
console.log(makeUser("ana"));
```

This prints `{ name: 'ana' }`. The same hazard applies to any `return` whose value begins on the next line; formatters that move the brace down are actively dangerous here.

Code review, please — this JavaScript code is meant to: run a setup callback once for each of three reserved slots.

```javascript
const slots = new Array(3);
let ran = 0;
slots.forEach(() => {
  ran++;
});
console.log(ran);
```

**Bug: `new Array(3)` creates holes, and array iteration methods skip holes.** The array has length 3 but no elements — indices 0..2 were never assigned. `forEach`, `map`, and friends visit only indices that actually exist, so the callback never runs at all. The array is not `[undefined, undefined, undefined]`; it is emptier than that, and `slots[0]` returning `undefined` hides the difference.

Failing case: the snippet prints `0`; the intent expects `3`.

Fix: create real elements, e.g. with `Array.from`:

```javascript
const slots = Array.from({ length: 3 });
let ran = 0;
slots.forEach(() => {
  ran++;
});
console.log(ran);
```

This prints `3`. (`fill(undefined)` also materializes the slots. A plain index `for` loop would have "worked" on the holey array too — one more reason this bug hides.)

Review the following JavaScript code. It is supposed to: remove the middle item from the queue.

```javascript
const queue = ["a", "b", "c"];
delete queue[1];
console.log(queue.length, queue[1]);
```

**Bug: `delete` on an array element removes the value but not the slot.** The array keeps length 3 with a hole at index 1. Anything that iterates by index now meets `undefined` mid-queue, `join` renders an empty field, and length-based logic (rotation, capacity checks) is silently off by the number of deletions.

Failing case: the snippet prints `3 undefined`; removing the middle item should leave length 2.

Fix: use `splice`, which shifts elements and adjusts the length:

```javascript
const queue = ["a", "b", "c"];
queue.splice(1, 1);
console.log(queue.length, queue[1]);
```

This prints `2 c`. `delete` is for object properties; arrays want `splice` (in place) or `filter` (new array).

Review this JavaScript snippet. Stated intent: pass the counter's increment method as a click callback and have it update the counter.

```javascript
const counter = {
  n: 0,
  inc() {
    this.n++;
  },
};
const handler = counter.inc;
handler();
console.log(counter.n);
```

**Bug: extracting a method detaches it from its receiver, so `this` is lost.** `counter.inc` is just a function value; called as plain `handler()`, its `this` is not `counter` (in sloppy mode it is the global object, in strict mode/modules `undefined`, where this line throws outright). The increment lands anywhere but on the counter.

Failing case: the snippet prints `0` — the counter never moved (and under strict semantics the call raises `TypeError` instead).

Fix: bind the receiver, or wrap the call:

```javascript
const counter = {
  n: 0,
  inc() {
    this.n++;
  },
};
const handler = counter.inc.bind(counter);
handler();
console.log(counter.n);
```

This prints `1`. An arrow-function property (`inc = () => { this.n++; }` in a class) captures the instance lexically and is the other common cure — but for object literals passed to event systems, `bind` at the hand-off point is the explicit fix.

Code review, please — this JavaScript code is meant to: parse a quantity typed by a user, which may contain a thousands separator.

```javascript
const qty = parseInt("1,000");
console.log(qty);
```

**Bug: `parseInt` stops at the first character it cannot use — here, the comma.** Parsing is prefix-based: it consumes `1`, meets `,`, and returns what it has. No error, no NaN, just a quantity silently 1000x too small, ready to flow into an order or a payment.

Failing case: the snippet prints `1`; the user typed one thousand.

Fix: normalize the input to the format actually accepted, and validate what remains:

```javascript
const raw = "1,000";
const qty = Number(raw.replace(/,/g, ""));
console.log(qty);
```

This prints `1000`. Note the general lesson cuts both ways: `parseInt("12abc")` is 12, not NaN — prefix parsers are for strings with known trailing junk (like `"12px"`), not for validating user numbers. For validation, `Number` plus a `Number.isNaN` check is the stricter tool.

Review the following JavaScript code. It is supposed to: skip records that have already been processed, comparing by their content.

```javascript
const processed = [{ id: 1 }, { id: 2 }];
const incoming = { id: 2 };
console.log(processed.includes(incoming));
```

**Bug: `includes` on objects compares by reference, not by content.** The array holds a *different object* that merely looks like `incoming`; identity comparison finds no match, so the record is processed a second time. Value-shaped data (parsed JSON, freshly built objects) almost never shares references, which makes this bug systematic rather than occasional.

Failing case: the snippet prints `false`, so record 2 would be reprocessed; the intent expects it to be recognised as seen.

Fix: track the identity that actually matters — the id — in a `Set`:

```javascript
const processedIds = new Set([1, 2]);
const incoming = { id: 2 };
console.log(processedIds.has(incoming.id));
```

This prints `true`. (For the general case, `processed.some((r) => r.id === incoming.id)` works without restructuring, at O(n) per check.)

Review this JavaScript snippet. Stated intent: remove every x marker from the list.

```javascript
const items = ["a", "x", "b", "x", "c"];
for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
  if (items[i] === "x") {
    items.splice(i, 1);
  }
}
console.log(items);
```

**Bug: splicing inside a forward loop skips the element that shifts into the vacated index.** After removing the first `x` at index 1, `b` slides into index 1 — but `i` advances to 2, landing on the second `x`'s old neighbour... concretely the second `x` is examined only by luck of positions; with adjacent targets (`["x", "x"]`) the skip is guaranteed. Here the surviving element is masked by the spacing.

Failing case: run the same loop on `["x", "x", "a"]` and it leaves `[ 'x', 'a' ]` — one marker survives. On the snippet's input it happens to print the right-looking `[ 'a', 'b', 'c' ]` only because the two markers are separated; the logic is still wrong.

Fix: iterate backwards when deleting in place, or filter into a new array:

```javascript
const items = ["a", "x", "b", "x", "c"];
const kept = items.filter((v) => v !== "x");
console.log(kept);
```

This prints `[ 'a', 'b', 'c' ]` for every arrangement, including adjacent markers.

Code review, please — this JavaScript code is meant to: freeze the configuration object so no part of it can be changed at runtime.

```javascript
const config = Object.freeze({ db: { host: "a" } });
config.db.host = "b";
console.log(config.db.host);
```

**Bug: `Object.freeze` is shallow.** It freezes the top level's properties — you cannot reassign `config.db` — but the nested `db` object itself is unfrozen, so `config.db.host` is freely writable. The code advertises an immutable config while the values that matter most (hosts, ports, feature flags nested one level down) remain mutable by any code holding a reference.

Failing case: the snippet prints `b`; the host was supposed to be locked at `a`.

Fix: freeze recursively:

```javascript
function deepFreeze(obj) {
  for (const v of Object.values(obj)) {
    if (v && typeof v === "object") deepFreeze(v);
  }
  return Object.freeze(obj);
}
const config = deepFreeze({ db: { host: "a" } });
config.db.host = "b";
console.log(config.db.host);
```

This prints `a` (in strict mode the assignment would throw; either way the value cannot change).

Review the following JavaScript code. It is supposed to: deep-copy an event object through JSON so it can be stored and its timestamp used later.

```javascript
const event = { at: new Date("2026-01-01T00:00:00Z") };
const stored = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(event));
console.log(typeof stored.at);
try {
  stored.at.getTime();
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.constructor.name + ": " + e.message);
}
```

**Bug: the JSON round-trip silently downgrades `Date` to string.** `JSON.stringify` calls `Date`'s `toJSON`, producing an ISO string; `JSON.parse` has no way to know it was ever a date, so `stored.at` is a plain string. Every other field survives, so the copy looks faithful until code calls a Date method on it — typically far from the copy site, in a reporting or expiry path.

Failing case: the snippet prints `string`, then a `TypeError` (`stored.at.getTime is not a function`).

Fix: revive the field explicitly, or use `structuredClone`, which understands `Date`:

```javascript
const event = { at: new Date("2026-01-01T00:00:00Z") };
const stored = structuredClone(event);
console.log(typeof stored.at, stored.at.getTime());
```

This prints `object 1767225600000`. If JSON storage is mandatory, revive on parse: `JSON.parse(text, (k, v) => (k === "at" ? new Date(v) : v))`. The same downgrade hits `undefined` (dropped), functions (dropped), `Map`/`Set` (emptied), and `BigInt` (throws) — JSON cloning is a serialization, not a copy.

