Code review, please — this JavaScript code is meant to: report how many keys an options object has, treating non-objects by their type name.

```javascript
function describe(v) {
  if (typeof v === "object") {
    return Object.keys(v).length + " keys";
  }
  return typeof v;
}
try {
  console.log(describe(null));
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.constructor.name + ": " + e.message);
}
```

**Bug: `typeof null` is `"object"` — a historical lie the guard walks straight into.** The check routes `null` into the object branch, and `Object.keys(null)` throws. This is among the oldest quirks in the language (a remnant of the original type-tag encoding) and it cannot be fixed at the language level without breaking the web, so every "is it an object" guard must special-case null forever.

Failing case: the snippet prints a `TypeError` (`Cannot convert undefined or null to object`) instead of a description.

Fix: exclude null explicitly:

```javascript
function describe(v) {
  if (v !== null && typeof v === "object") {
    return Object.keys(v).length + " keys";
  }
  return v === null ? "null" : typeof v;
}
console.log(describe(null));
```

This prints `null`, and real objects still report their key count. (Arrays also pass this guard — `typeof []` is `"object"` too; if the intent excludes them, add an `Array.isArray` check rather than trusting `typeof` further.)

Review the following JavaScript code. It is supposed to: accept a booking date from year/month/day fields and store it.

```javascript
const y = 2026, m = 2, d = 30;
const booking = new Date(y, m - 1, d);
console.log(booking.toDateString());
```

**Bug: the `Date` constructor rolls out-of-range fields over instead of rejecting them.** February 30 does not exist, but the constructor normalizes the overflow into March 2. Code that treats successful construction as validation has accepted an impossible booking and silently moved it to a different day — the customer asked for late February and is booked in March.

Failing case: the snippet prints `Mon Mar 02 2026`; the intent expects Feb 30 to be rejected as invalid.

Fix: validate by round-tripping the components — a real date reconstructs to itself:

```javascript
function makeDate(y, m, d) {
  const dt = new Date(y, m - 1, d);
  if (dt.getFullYear() !== y || dt.getMonth() !== m - 1 || dt.getDate() !== d) {
    return null;
  }
  return dt;
}
console.log(makeDate(2026, 2, 30));
console.log(makeDate(2026, 2, 28).toDateString());
```

This prints `null` for the impossible date and `Sat Feb 28 2026` for the real one. The same rollover applies to month 13, day 0, and hour 25 — construction is never validation in this API.

