Review the following Go code. It is supposed to: apply a 2x price adjustment to every item in the catalogue.

```go
package main

import "fmt"

type Item struct{ Price int }

func main() {
    items := []Item{{Price: 10}, {Price: 20}}
    for _, it := range items {
        it.Price *= 2
    }
    fmt.Println(items)
}
```

**Bug: the range variable is a copy of each element, not a reference to it.** `for _, it := range items` copies each struct into `it`; doubling `it.Price` modifies the copy, which is discarded at the end of the iteration. The slice itself is never written. (With a slice of pointers the same loop would work — the copy would be of the pointer, and the write would go through it — which is why this bug appears and disappears as types change.)

Failing case: the program prints `[{10} {20}]`; the intent expects `[{20} {40}]`.

Fix: range over indices and write through the slice:

```go
package main

import "fmt"

type Item struct{ Price int }

func main() {
    items := []Item{{Price: 10}, {Price: 20}}
    for i := range items {
        items[i].Price *= 2
    }
    fmt.Println(items)
}
```

This prints `[{20} {40}]`.

Review this Go snippet. Stated intent: process each file and close it when done with it, before moving to the next.

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "os"
)

func process(paths []string) {
    for _, p := range paths {
        f, err := os.Open(p)
        if err != nil {
            continue
        }
        defer f.Close()
        fmt.Println("processing", p)
    }
    fmt.Println("loop finished")
}

func main() {
    process([]string{"a.txt", "b.txt", "c.txt"})
}
```

**Bug: `defer` runs when the *function* returns, not when the loop iteration ends.** Every opened file stays open for the whole loop; the closes all happen in a pile at the end of `process`. With a handful of files that is merely untidy. With thousands, the process accumulates open descriptors until the OS refuses more (`too many open files`) — and it fails partway through real work, on the largest inputs, which is the worst possible time.

Failing case: at the moment "loop finished" prints, all three files are still open; on a large directory the function dies mid-loop with descriptor exhaustion instead of closing as it goes.

Fix: close explicitly at the end of each iteration (or give each file its own function scope so `defer` means what it looks like):

```go
func processOne(p string) {
    f, err := os.Open(p)
    if err != nil {
        return
    }
    defer f.Close()
    fmt.Println("processing", p)
}

func process(paths []string) {
    for _, p := range paths {
        processOne(p)
    }
    fmt.Println("loop finished")
}
```

`defer` is the right tool at function scope; in a loop body it is a resource leak wearing a tidy disguise.

Code review, please — this Go code is meant to: return a JSON response whose tags field is always an array, even when a post has no tags.

```go
package main

import (
    "encoding/json"
    "fmt"
)

func main() {
    var tags []string
    body, _ := json.Marshal(map[string]any{"tags": tags})
    fmt.Println(string(body))
}
```

**Bug: a nil slice marshals to `null`, not `[]`.** `var tags []string` declares a nil slice; `encoding/json` renders nil slices (and maps) as `null`. The API contract says array, and strongly-typed clients — and plenty of JavaScript ones doing `tags.length` or `tags.map` — break on the null. The Go side compiles and unit-tests fine because a nil slice behaves like an empty one for `len` and `range`; only the wire format differs.

Failing case: the program prints `{"tags":null}`; the intent expects `{"tags":[]}`.

Fix: initialize the slice to a non-nil empty value:

```go
package main

import (
    "encoding/json"
    "fmt"
)

func main() {
    tags := []string{}
    body, _ := json.Marshal(map[string]any{"tags": tags})
    fmt.Println(string(body))
}
```

This prints `{"tags":[]}`. The general rule for response structs: initialize every slice and map field at construction, or normalize nil to empty in one place before marshaling.

Review this Go snippet. Stated intent: report the average of the scores.

```go
